From: =?UTF-8?q?Lajosh=C3=A1zi=2C=20L=C3=A1szl=C3=B3=20Gergely?= <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] camera: only a tap on the picture takes a picture

In Tap mode a tap on the viewfinder takes the photograph. The controls,
though, are laid over the viewfinder in a layer that spans the whole
page, and the gesture that reads the tap is on the page rather than on
the viewfinder - it has to be, or the overlay would swallow every touch.
So a tap on the menu, on the shutter bar, or on the thumbnail that opens
the gallery arrived at that gesture exactly like a tap on the picture,
and fired the shutter on the way past.

The thumbnail is the worst of the three: it takes a photograph while
leaving the camera, so the picture that gets saved is whatever the phone
happened to be pointing at as the finger moved.

Ask what the tap landed on before acting on it. Both bars are a
GtkCenterBox and everything that answers a press is either inside one of
them or a control in its own right, while the picture lies underneath
all of it, so walking up from the picked widget separates the two. The
page has to be showing the camera as well: a tap that arrives while
another page is up was never aimed here.

Signed-off-by: Lajosházi, László Gergely <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/src/widgets/camera.rs
+++ b/src/widgets/camera.rs
@@ -165,6 +165,60 @@
                 .get_or_init(|| gio::Settings::new(config::APP_ID))
         }
 
+        /// Whether a tap at this point was aimed at the picture itself.
+        ///
+        /// The controls are laid over the viewfinder in a layer that spans the
+        /// whole page, so a tap on the menu, on the shutter bar or on the
+        /// thumbnail that opens the gallery reaches this widget exactly like a
+        /// tap on the picture does. Taking a photograph from any of those is
+        /// wrong twice over: it is not what the finger was pointing at, and in
+        /// the thumbnail's case it fires a shutter on the way out of the
+        /// camera.
+        ///
+        /// So ask what the tap actually landed on. Both bars are a
+        /// `GtkCenterBox`, and everything that answers a press lives inside
+        /// one of them or is a control in its own right; the picture is
+        /// underneath all of it. Anything that names itself is not the
+        /// picture.
+        fn tap_hits_the_picture(&self, x: f64, y: f64) -> bool {
+            let obj = self.obj();
+
+            if self.stack.visible_child_name().as_deref() != Some("camera") {
+                return false;
+            }
+
+            /* Nothing was picked where the layer is not there at all. */
+            let Some(picked) = obj.pick(x, y, gtk::PickFlags::DEFAULT) else {
+                return true;
+            };
+
+            let mut widget = Some(picked.clone());
+            while let Some(current) = widget {
+                if current.eq(obj.upcast_ref::<gtk::Widget>()) {
+                    break;
+                }
+
+                if current.is::<gtk::CenterBox>()
+                    || current.is::<gtk::Button>()
+                    || current.is::<gtk::MenuButton>()
+                    || current.is::<gtk::ToggleButton>()
+                    || current.is::<gtk::Scale>()
+                    || current.is::<gtk::Popover>()
+                    || current.is::<crate::CameraControls>()
+                {
+                    log::debug!(
+                        "Tap at {x}x{y} landed on {}, not on the picture",
+                        current.type_().name()
+                    );
+                    return false;
+                }
+
+                widget = current.parent();
+            }
+
+            true
+        }
+
         /// The Stop button on the recalibration overlay.
